ges .", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-29T14:55:21Z", "username": "Pavan Pamidimarri", "text": "Nilesh: 25, Yash: 50, Akshay: 5, Nikhil: 35, Srijan: 65, Shyam: 5", "role": "hint", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-29T14:55:25Z", "username": "Pavan Pamidimarri", "text": "last question", "role": "hint", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-29T14:55:34Z", "username": "Pavan Pamidimarri", "text": "12.\tThe Latin verb ______ means “to do” or “to make,” and its past participle, ______, means “a thing done” or “a thing made.” English borrowed this past participle three times, and the three resulting words are all so common that you may not notice their shared root. The first borrowing X came in the 14th century via Old French. It originally meant simply “an act” or “a deed,” but narrowed in English to mean an impressive act — usually one requiring skill, strength, or daring. The second borrowing Y came directly from Latin in the 16th century. It originally meant “an act done,” but in English it drifted away from the act itself toward the idea of something that has demonstrably occurred — and from there to “something that is the case, something verifiably true.” The third borrowing Z came via Old French, originally meaning “the way something is made, its form.” In English it was first applied to the form of the human face, then generalized to any prominent characteristic of anything. Z is used today, among other things, to list the capabilities of any software product. ID X (5 points), Y (5 points), and Z (5 points).", "role": "hint", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-29T14:55:51Z", "username": "Yash Agrawal", "text": "feat", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-29T14:55:51Z", "username": "Anurag Bhide", "text": "Deed", "rol